ght: start with the 7-card straight (8-A). It\'s the highest possible straight in this game, guaranteeing that it can only be bombed. This *forces* the opponent\'s hand and establishes control. It\'s essentially an opening salvo that gets 7 cards off the table. This is the optimal strategic move and secures the initiative.\n\nPost-straight, the plan is to utilize the Quadplex Set (3333-55-66). This is a great discard, leveraging the small bomb to get rid of lower value cards, leaving the remaining hand very manageable. I\'m left with the Pair, Single, and Rocket.\n\nThe risk? That someone beats the straight with a bomb. In this case, I\'d still have the 3333 bomb and the rocket. But, the straight is the better play, it\'s virtually unbeatable. My thinking is to ensure the straight is the first move.\n\nAlternative moves are not as effective, the "quadplex-combo" first is too risky and can be bombed. Using the straight first maintains initiative. The strategy is to dump the "blockable" hand (3333-55-66) after the straight, so this is my final move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand structure is excellent, consisting of a Rocket (BlackJoker, RedJoker), a single 2, a pair of 9s, a Quadplex set (3333 + 55 + 66), and a 7-card Straight (8-9-10-J-Q-K-A). The Straight 8-A is the highest possible straight of this length (as 2s are not allowed in straights), making it unbeatable by any normal hand, only by bombs. Playing this first guarantees maintaining initiative (or forcing out a bomb) while clearing a large portion of the hand. The remaining cards (3333-55-66, 99, 2, Rocket) form a very strong winning path.\n\nReason: The Straight 8-A is the "nuts" (highest possible) for its category. Playing it first maximizes the chance of keeping the lead to subsequently play the 3333-55-66 Quadplex set, which clears the smaller pairs.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦8", "♦9", "♣10", "♣J", "♠Q", "♠K", "♦A"]}'}
